```c
			rc = -EEXIST;
			goto out;
```
Reason: After a successful call to `ksmbd_vfs_kern_path_start_removing()`, when either `ksmbd_vfs_remove_file()` fails (inside the `ReplaceIfExists` branch) or the link already exists (`ReplaceIfExists` not set), the error handling jumps directly to the `out` label without calling `ksmbd_vfs_kern_path_end_removing()`. This skips releasing the inode locks and dropping the path references, leading to unbalanced locks and potential deadlocks, as shown in the kernel warning.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
		ksmbd_vfs_kern_path_end_removing(&path);
		if (rc)
			goto out;
```
Reason: The call to `ksmbd_vfs_kern_path_end_removing(&path)` is moved before any `goto out` so that it is always executed after the inner `if-else` block. Then, if `rc` is non-zero (e.g., -EINVAL or -EEXIST), the function returns via `out` with the locks properly released. This ensures balanced lock/unlock semantics and prevents the reported workqueue lock leak.

